          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I had flu two weeks ago and I am still exhausted. Is prolonged tiredness afterwards a concern, and what does guidance describe about antivirals and vaccination?",
          "answer": "If your symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, you should seek medical advice (diagnostic).\n\nThe information available does not describe whether prolonged tiredness two weeks after influenza is a concern (research). Influenza typically presents with marked fatigue at the onset (condition-influenza, research). No emergency pattern in the red-flag table matched this description. This does not mean it cannot be serious, as the table covers a fixed list of presentations, and a symptom it does not list can still be serious (diagnostic). For most otherwise healthy people, managing the flu involves supportive care such as rest, fluids, and symptom relief (condition-influenza, diagnostic). However, other complications of influenza can include worsening of underlying conditions like asthma, COPD, heart failure, or diabetes. Less commonly, there can be inflammation of the heart muscle, brain, or muscle tissue. The flu is also associated with a temporary increase in the risk of heart attack and stroke in the weeks following the infection (condition-influenza, diagnostic).\n\nRegarding antivirals, treatment is most effective when started within 48 hours of symptoms beginning, and its benefit decreases after this window (guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals, diagnostic). It is recommended as early as possible for anyone hospitalized with suspected or confirmed influenza, anyone with severe, complicated, or progressive illness, and anyone at higher risk of complications (guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals, diagnostic). For severely ill patients, observational evidence supports the benefit of antivirals (guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals, research). For low-risk outpatients, guidance differs: some sources suggest antivirals as a reasonable option to reduce symptoms, while others are more cautious, noting that the effect on symptom duration is modest, the benefit for preventing complications or hospitalization is not well established in this group, and routine use is not justified (guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals, research). Antibiotics are not effective against influenza and are used only for bacterial complications (guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals, research).\n\nFor vaccination, annual influenza vaccination is recommended for everyone aged six months and older who does not have a contraindication, particularly for those at higher risk of complications (guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals, diagnostic). These higher-risk groups include young children, pregnant people, adults aged 65 and older, and individuals with certain chronic medical conditions (guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals, diagnostic). Annual influenza vaccination is considered an important measure for people with conditions like cardiovascular disease and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD); it is included as a cardiovascular measure, not just a respiratory one, and it helps reduce exacerbations in people with COPD (guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention, guideline-copd-diagnosis-and-stepwise-management, research).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
